  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 200 AM MDT Thu Sep 10 2026\n\nAnother day of critical fire weather conditions returns across the \nCWA today. RH values ranging between 7 to 15 percent will occur for \nmost lower elevation locations this afternoon. Model forecasts for \nwind gusts have trended slightly downward for this afternoon. Winds \nwill steadily increase through the afternoon, with gusts of 25 to 30 \nmph occurring over western portions of the forecast area and from \nthe southwestern corner of the state to Natrona County. The higher \ngusts of 35 mph will be more confined to the eastern slopes of the \nAbsaroka and Wind River Mountains. Burning of any kind continues to \nbe highly discouraged! Otherwise, today will also be the warmest day \nof the week, with highs in the middle to upper 80s east of the \nDivide. Readings in the middle to upper 70s will occur west of the \nDivide. \n\nClouds will begin to increase from west to east through the \nafternoon. This will gradually lead to a chance for virga showers \nthis evening into Friday morning. The central basins look to have \nthe best chances between 01Z and 06Z with the main impact being \ngusty outflow winds. Winds will stay elevated for most areas and \nbegin to decrease after 03Z Friday (9pm Thursday), with gusts up to \n35 mph continuing over the east slopes of the Absaroka and Wind \nRiver Mountains through the rest of the night. Isolated virga \nshowers will become possible over western portions by 12Z Friday \nmorning and end by 18Z. These showers will be part of a larger push \nof moisture from UT/ID moving over the Cowboy State. This influx of \nmoisture will also be the cause why humidity values will not drop \nbelow the critical threshold Friday afternoon. \n\nThe timing for the cold front has been pushed toward late afternoon, \nmoving through Jackson, Cody and Kaycee by 00Z Saturday (6pm \nFriday). Temperatures will be slightly cooler east of the Divide as \na result, especially for northern portions of the CWA. Widespread \nwind gusts of 30 to 35 mph remain in the forecast for Friday, \nkeeping fire weather conditions elevated. The front will make it to \nthe Divide by 06Z Saturday, becoming stationary through the rest of \nthe night into Saturday morning.  \n\nThe forecast for the weekend storm system has shifted dramatically \nthis run. The storm system will drop southward along the West Coast \nFriday night into Saturday, a track that is much further west than \nprevious runs. This will cause the aforementioned cold front to \nretreat northward Saturday afternoon, due to an increase in \nsouthwesterly winds. Initial indications show the front getting \npushed into the Bighorn Basin and Johnson County by Saturday \nevening. Conditions are expected to remain dry until Sunday morning, \nas the storm begins to track over the Northern Rockies. \nPrecipitation looks to stay confined over northern portions through \nthe day Sunday as the storm stays further north in MT. The associated \ncold front will push through the Cowboy state Sunday night, clearing \nthe forecast area by 12Z Monday. Sub-freezing temperatures will \nreturn to western portions, with readings in the upper 30s to lower \n40s elsewhere. Dry and cooler conditions will be in place Monday, \nwith highs in the 60s to near 70 degrees across the area. Another \ncold front looks to clip northern portions on Wednesday.",
